    "solution": "Let $A = \\lim_{k\\to\\infty} \\left\\lvert \\frac{\\ka_{k+1}}{\\ka_k} \\right\\rvert$. The differentiated series is $\\sum_{k=1}^\\infty k \\ka_k x^{k-1}$; reindexing ($k \\to k+1$) writes it as $\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\kb_k x^k$ with $\\kb_k = (k+1)\\ka_{k+1}$. Then $\\left\\lvert \\frac{\\kb_{k+1}}{\\kb_k} \\right\\rvert = \\frac{k+2}{k+1} \\left\\lvert \\frac{\\ka_{k+2}}{\\ka_{k+1}} \\right\\rvert$. As $k \\to \\infty$, $\\frac{k+2}{k+1} \\to 1$ and $\\left\\lvert \\frac{\\ka_{k+2}}{\\ka_{k+1}} \\right\\rvert \\to A$ (the same limit, one index later), so the limit for the differentiated series is $1 \\cdot A = A$. By Theorem 7.1.2 both series have radius of convergence $1/A$, or $\\infty$ when $A = 0$; if $A$ diverges to $\\infty$, both are divergent."
